{"canonical_record":{"canonical_payload_sha256":"04e7ff903b1e968c6f432a72cb4e32239001ee9bf95bafb822dc76ff76caa461","full_review_record_id":"fullrev_63e0ea223497a65710f638108245ff2846d80449f440f576be2d5e63a700615f","overall_assessment":"The paper presents a coherent theoretical framework connecting Tensorial Mixture Models to Convolutional Arithmetic Circuits, with a clear motivation for handling missing data. The experimental evaluation is limited to small-scale datasets (MNIST, NORB) and lacks statistical rigor, such as confidence intervals or multiple runs, which weakens the empirical support for the claimed performance gaps. Reproducibility is hindered by the absence of code and specific hyperparameter details.","paper_id":"BJluGHcee","paper_identity_sha256":"73ba9986b2f2dcb5cd3e7ab20989db1baa0c78af4c124fe1907a1a67479d8e61","prompt_identity_sha256":"095ad7b0a00063602140e74f69656ff1f0ecec56173ae4e7f32379231184d0fa","raw_response_sha256":"d93a1c684b04b33642f2be9060fb2a5c311237c38b832860a323e232423a3d1c","request_identity_sha256":"e135acf042e93409f60e7bbd1be7a295f3a6bf9130e09f6ac1d87128c95b41ee","reviewer_identity_sha256":"32b29c26fbf1508e066dfb25b1a9a3fe0654c5869a7da3020e1e8e61940befe3","role":"QUAL","schema_version":"agentreview-full-review-corpus-v4","structural_context_sha256":"a50f84348000f243f0aa04741888d7599ca429fa5c2e813413593c9c8b95d14f","subdimension_sections":[{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-016","SRC-017","SRC-018","SRC-019","SRC-020","SRC-021","SRC-022","SRC-023","SRC-024","SRC-025","SRC-026","SRC-027","SRC-028","SRC-029","SRC-030","SRC-031","SRC-032","SRC-033","SRC-034","SRC-035","SRC-036","SRC-037","SRC-038","SRC-039","SRC-040"],"review_text":"The methodological formulation of Tensorial Mixture Models (TMMs) is mathematically consistent. The paper correctly identifies the intractability of the full priors tensor and proposes tensor decompositions (CP and HT) to render inference tractable via Convolutional Arithmetic Circuits (ConvACs). The derivation of the generative loss and its combination with the discriminative loss for classification is logically sound. The special cases, such as the reduction to diagonal Gaussian Mixture Models and bag-of-words models, are correctly specified, demonstrating a clear understanding of the model's boundaries. The theoretical link between the simplex constraints of the priors tensor and the non-negativity of the decomposition parameters is well-established, ensuring the resulting model remains a valid probability distribution.","subdimension_id":"QUAL-MET"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-001","SRC-002","SRC-003","SRC-004","SRC-005","SRC-006","SRC-007","SRC-008","SRC-009","SRC-010","SRC-011","SRC-012","SRC-013","SRC-014","SRC-015"],"review_text":"The experimental design focuses on classification with missing data, using MNIST and NORB datasets. While the choice of datasets is appropriate for demonstrating the handling of missing values, the evaluation is limited in scope, lacking tests on more complex or larger-scale benchmarks that would better validate the scalability claims of the ConvAC architecture. The baselines include data imputation methods (GSN, NICE, DPM) and discriminative ConvNets, which are relevant. However, the comparison to generative models is somewhat limited by the authors' admission that some recent methods were excluded due to code availability, which may introduce a selection bias in the reported performance gaps. The ablation study comparing the shallow GCP model to the deep GHT model is useful but could be expanded to include other architectural variations.","subdimension_id":"QUAL-EXP"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-001","SRC-002","SRC-003","SRC-004","SRC-005","SRC-006","SRC-007","SRC-008","SRC-009","SRC-010","SRC-011","SRC-012","SRC-013","SRC-014","SRC-015"],"review_text":"The paper lacks sufficient detail for full reproducibility. There is no mention of code availability, which is a significant barrier for replicating the ConvAC implementation. Specific hyperparameters, such as learning rates, batch sizes, and the exact number of channels or pooling window sizes used for the MNIST and NORB experiments, are not provided in the main text or the visible sections. The training procedure is described in general terms (stochastic gradient descent), but the specific optimization settings and regularization techniques are not detailed. This omission makes it difficult for other researchers to verify the reported results or to build upon the proposed architecture.","subdimension_id":"QUAL-REP"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-001","SRC-002","SRC-003","SRC-004","SRC-005","SRC-006","SRC-007","SRC-008","SRC-009","SRC-010","SRC-011","SRC-012","SRC-013","SRC-014","SRC-015"],"review_text":"The paper provides a thorough comparison to prior work in the context of generative models and missing data. It effectively positions TMMs against discriminative models, variational inference methods, and GANs, highlighting the unique advantage of tractable marginalization. The comparison to Sum-Product Networks is well-articulated, noting the differences in structural design and training procedures. However, the comparison to recent state-of-the-art inpainting or missing data classification methods is limited by the exclusion of some works due to code unavailability, which may not fully represent the current state of the art. The literature review is comprehensive in covering the theoretical underpinnings of tensor decompositions and their application to generative models.","subdimension_id":"QUAL-CMP"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-001","SRC-002","SRC-003","SRC-004","SRC-005","SRC-006","SRC-007","SRC-008","SRC-009","SRC-010","SRC-011","SRC-012","SRC-013","SRC-014","SRC-015"],"review_text":"The statistical rigor of the experimental results is limited. The paper reports accuracy metrics for various missing data scenarios but does not provide confidence intervals, standard deviations, or results from multiple independent runs. This makes it difficult to assess the statistical significance of the reported performance improvements, particularly the large gaps claimed over baseline methods. The lack of variance reporting is a notable weakness, as it prevents a robust evaluation of the model's stability and reliability. The use of single-run results for such a significant claim of superiority requires more rigorous statistical validation to be convincing.","subdimension_id":"QUAL-STA"}]},"disposition":"CANONICAL_FULL_REVIEW_COMMITTED","item_commit_sha256":"be26962f3076cef2ef8e3178bd009c61b058e9ac0e5a08bee61c3ea241c22f75","paper_id":"BJluGHcee","paper_ordinal":35,"raw_attempts":[{"assistant_content_base64":"","assistant_content_sha256":"d93a1c684b04b33642f2be9060fb2a5c311237c38b832860a323e232423a3d1c","attempt_index":0,"error":"","finish_reason":"stop","paper_id":"BJluGHcee","raw_provider_response_base64":"","raw_provider_response_sha256":"07634ae9daf46c84e0c3c317e1763f4bd1b53ac2e5176f4ac004a495d279888d","request_identity_sha256":"e135acf042e93409f60e7bbd1be7a295f3a6bf9130e09f6ac1d87128c95b41ee","role":"QUAL","schema_version":"no-memory-full-review-corpus-build-v6","usage":{"completion_tokens":1759,"prompt_tokens":17459,"prompt_tokens_details":null,"total_tokens":19218},"valid":true}],"request_identity_sha256":"e135acf042e93409f60e7bbd1be7a295f3a6bf9130e09f6ac1d87128c95b41ee","role":"QUAL","role_ordinal":0,"schema_version":"corpus-build-bootstrap-checkpoint-v4"}